---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const posts = (await getCollection("writing", ({ data }) => !data.draft))
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf())
  .slice(0, 10);

const fmt = (d: Date) =>
  d.toLocaleDateString("en-US", {
    year: "numeric",
    month: "short",
    day: "numeric",
  });
---

<Base>
  <p class="cmd"><span class="you">andrew@pope</span> ~ <span class="sigil">$</span> whoami</p>

  <h1>Andrew Pope<span class="cursor"></span></h1>
  <p class="muted">
    amateur artificer | bounded rationalist | perennial tinkerer
  </p>

  <p class="cmd">
    <span class="you">andrew@pope</span> ~ <span class="sigil">$</span> ls latest/
  </p>
  {
    posts.length === 0 ? (
      <p class="muted">Nothing published yet.</p>
    ) : (
      <ul class="entry-list">
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/writing/${post.id}`} class="entry-title">
              {post.data.title}
            </a>
            <span class="entry-meta"> — {fmt(post.data.date)}</span>
            {post.data.description && (
              <p class="entry-desc">{post.data.description}</p>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )
  }

  <p class="muted" style="margin-top:2rem;">
    Go deeper: <a href="/projects">projects</a> | <a href="/lab">lab</a> |
    <a href="/writing">writing</a>
  </p>

  <p class="cmd" style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
    <span class="you">andrew@pope</span> ~ <span class="sigil">$</span> cat about.txt
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    I'm deeply curious about how things work, and I've always loved fixing
    them. In my day job I'm an Air Force RPA pilot, teaching new lieutenants to
    fly remotely — and over my career I've been an aircraft maintainer, a
    recruiter, and an instructor at the Air Force Weapons School. I make apps
    too. This is where I write about all of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Currently I've been revisiting semiconductors and basic computers. I plan to go as far into this rabbit hole as my self-guided education will take me. I love finding ways where the world of software collides with hardware and engineering constraints. I've got a few cool projects in mind and there's never been a better time to learn and build.
  </p>

  <p class="cmd">
    <span class="you">andrew@pope</span> ~ <span class="sigil">$</span> cat now.txt
  </p>
  <ul class="entry-list">
    <li>
      <span class="entry-title">Basic Computing</span>
      <p class="entry-desc">
        Sometimes when I build other apps, I've found myself wondering what's going on down there at the bare metal layer. This is my inquiry into just that: how do computers work? And in learning, what can I apply to other projects?
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="entry-title">Circuit Box</span>
      <span class="badge">building</span>
      <p class="entry-desc">
        An interactive logic-circuit builder that climbs the full ladder of abstraction — one gate up to a working 8-bit CPU. A way to do some hands on learning as I progress through my computing course.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="entry-title">Toy Soldiers</span>
      <p class="entry-desc">
        A study of the origin of the components on a tiny whoop flight controller board. A tiny whoop is a small quadcopter (&lt;20g typically) with integrated radio controls and a video transmitter. A small miracle of embedded systems, the tiny whoop also demonstrates where so many of our chips come from.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p class="cmd">
    <span class="you">andrew@pope</span> ~ <span class="sigil">$</span> cat why.txt
  </p>
  <p>
    What do I believe at my core? A lot of things really, but in this space: that we have an imperative to build. I've noticed a distinct polarization in the world. In one view, there's a sort of creeping nihilism at work. A sense that people are helpless to change things around them. In another view, a techno-optimism that may be a little blind to the risks and humanity's history with unequally distributed outcomes. So where does that leave me? I'm a bit of a dreary optimist. I'm not sure how this all turns out, that's a matter of faith. But in the meantime, we must not bury our talents.
  </p>
</Base>
